shutil.copy2(R/'tools/ca_soft_rules_model.js',O/'ca-soft-rules-model.js')
save(E/'runtime-receipt.json',json.dumps(receipt,indent=2));save(E/'runtime-verification.json',json.dumps(result,indent=2))
tree=ast.parse((R/'tools/build_ca_history_review.py').read_text(encoding='utf-8'));css=next(ast.literal_eval(n.value) for n in tree.body if isinstance(n,ast.Assign) and any(isinstance(t,ast.Name) and t.id=='css' for t in n.targets))
css+='''.fields{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr) minmax(0,1fr);gap:22px}.fields canvas{width:100%;height:auto;display:block;image-rendering:pixelated;border:1px solid #779391;border-radius:3px}.fields .wall{margin-top:18px;aspect-ratio:4/3;object-fit:fill;border-top:8px solid #c7a776;border-radius:12% 12% 2px 2px}.wall[hidden]{display:none}.fields h3{font-size:clamp(15px,2.5vw,25px)}.fields p{font-size:15px;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}.instrument{padding:18px;background:#203e43;border-left:3px solid #ffcc70}.controls button[aria-pressed=true]{background:#d8b1c7;color:#142c36}.reading h1{font-size:36px}.reading pre{overflow:auto}.reading code{white-space:pre}.prompt{padding:18px;background:#243e46}'''
body=markdown.markdown((M/'final.md').read_text(),extensions=['fenced_code'])
html='''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>What can a cell receive? · Ada Research</title><style>__CSS__</style></head><body><main><nav><a href="learning-ladder.html">← Learning ladder</a><a href="ca-proximity.html">Previous: the surface includes you</a><a href="/necklace/thread?map=CA_SoftRules&role=primary">Primary &amp; book</a></nav><div class="eyebrow">Cellular automata / CA_SoftRules</div><h1>What can a cell receive?</h1><p class="lead">Two identical beginnings. One field exchanges material with its neighbours; the other keeps only its local reaction. Follow the first difference into a room.</p><p class="status">Built in Godot · __CHECKS__ desktop checks passed. All six authored works now load, with two primary book passages. Headset and retained-secondary review remain for later.</p><figure><img src="ca-soft-rules-exchange.png" alt="Actual Godot museum: paired curved field walls and table displays, showing different outcomes after a thousand updates"><figcaption>Actual museum at tick 1,000. A and B share their beginning, reaction, feed and removal. Only neighbourhood exchange differs.</figcaption></figure><section><h2>Predict the first arrival</h2><p class="prompt">Find the gold-framed address W. It starts just outside the seeded square. STEP once. Which field can give it its first V?</p><p class="small">Remote model of the installed arithmetic. The photographs above and below show the actual Godot hall. This model exposes the same 32² fields, fixed beginning, periodic edges and controls.</p><div class="controls"><button id="step">STEP</button><button id="run">RUN</button><button id="replay">REPLAY</button><button id="hundred">+100</button><button id="exchange" aria-pressed="false">EXCHANGE B</button><button id="inject">INJECT</button><button id="view" aria-pressed="false">VIEW U/V</button><button id="coat" aria-pressed="true">COAT</button></div><p class="instrument" id="model-clock" aria-live="polite"></p><p class="small" id="model-view"></p><div class="fields"><article><h3>A · exchange ON</h3><canvas id="field-0" width="384" height="384" aria-label="Field A table projection"></canvas><p id="witness-a"></p><canvas id="wall-0" class="wall" width="384" height="384" aria-label="The same A field on a wall"></canvas></article><article><h3 id="model-b-title">B · exchange OFF</h3><canvas id="field-1" width="384" height="384" aria-label="Field B table projection"></canvas><p id="witness-b"></p><canvas id="wall-1" class="wall" width="384" height="384" aria-label="The same B field on a wall"></canvas></article></div><p class="small" id="coat-note"></p><details><summary>Make a fair comparison</summary><p>EXCHANGE B restores both beginnings. With exchange ON in both, all addresses agree at matching ticks. OFF preserves reaction, feed and removal but removes both diffusion terms from B. At W, one update gives A: U=.920, V=.020; B: U=1.000, V=.000.</p><p>+100 schedules a hundred further updates. INJECT edits the same small patch in both present fields. REPLAY clears interventions and restores the beginning while retaining your exchange and display choices. VIEW and COAT leave state and time unchanged. RUN does at most four updates per 0.1-second opportunity; numerical ticks are not wall-clock seconds. Both fields hold at tick 2,400.</p></details></section><section><h2>From participation to exchange</h2><p>The bridge counted neighbours above a health threshold. Here the amount of difference matters: two concentrations, a local reaction, and exchange through four neighbours. The new body is made possible by a specific relation we can switch and inspect.</p><p>The table and curved wall use the same field texture. A change in material can transform the experience of the room while its support stays fixed. What else would we have to implement before the field could become a force, a growing surface or an input to another body?</p><figure><img src="ca-soft-rules-walls.png" alt="Inside the paired field bay with curved walls and the side instrument"><figcaption>A walkable centre and side-mounted instrument keep the changing surfaces available to view.</figcaption></figure></section><section><details><summary>Read the book passage</summary><div class="reading">__BODY__</div></details></section><section><h2>The diagram remains</h2><figure><img src="ca-soft-rules-clockmaker.png" alt="Actual restored clockmaker bench with brass stencils, buffer trays and small cellular diagrams"><figcaption>The clockmaker is the second primary. Its ornamental crank and fractional ring now carry descriptions that distinguish a still illustration from a live field.</figcaption></figure><p>The old museum plan carried only two of the six map works. The rebuilt plan restores the authored collection, and a missing registry flag is repaired so the clockmaker actually loads. No authored work was removed.</p><details><summary>Photographs, controls and evidence</summary><figure><img src="ca-soft-rules-entrance.png" alt="The held beginning in the actual museum"></figure><figure><img src="ca-soft-rules-controls.png" alt="Eight real Godot controls on the mounted side panel"></figure><figure><img src="ca-soft-rules-readout.png" alt="Mounted readout showing actual U and V at W with tick and exchange status"></figure><figure><img src="ca-soft-rules-whole-hall.png" alt="Overhead view of the study bay, clockmaker and retained gallery"></figure><p>__CHECKS__ desktop checks: initial equality, independently calculated first values, periodic edges, agreement with the existing batch solver, matched coupling, shared GPU texture values, all eight real pointer controls, continuous support and two physical walks. No runtime script errors. The existing startup UID diagnostic remains.</p><p><a href="ca-soft-rules-technical.md">Technical account</a> · <a href="ca-soft-rules-critical.md">Critical text</a> · <a href="ca-soft-rules-field_notes.md">Field notes</a> · <a href="ca-soft-rules-verification.json">Verification</a></p></details></section><footer class="small"><a href="ca-soft-rules-final.md">Book source</a> · Next: CA_EdgeOfChaos. Carry the matched beginning and visible clock into a test of intervention.</footer></main><script src="ca-soft-rules-model.js"></script></body></html>'''
